  "query_preview": "..\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nA strong upper low is over southern Saskatchewan early this\nmorning. The low will move northeast into Manitoba today.\nMeanwhile, a strong shortwave trough over the Pacific NW early\nthis morning will move east across western Montana this morning\nand through eastern Montana this afternoon and evening bring\nshowers and thunderstorms with it. \n\nStrong west winds are expected tonight and Tuesday with the\nexception of decoupling at times tonight allow for lighter winds\nin some areas. The higher elevations of the Little Rockies should\nhave the strongest winds tonight where 50 knots 850 mb winds could\nbring gusts to 60 mph at times. Issued a High Wind Warning for SW\nPhillips County until 9 am. Will also continue a Lake Wind\nAdvisory until midnight tonight. \n\nWinds will diminish tonight as surface high pressure moves into\nthe area.  \nLook for sunshine and warmer temperatures on Wednesday with highs \nnear normal, mid 70s to near 80 for the CWA.\n\nOn Thursday, the next disturbance moves east from western Canada\nwith a cold front approaching the CWA. Temps should reach near 80\nacross the northern zones and possibly near 90 in the southeast\nzones. With Min RH in the teens and an increasing West wind, fire\nweather highlights may need to be considered.\n\nEnsembles then show a much cooler period expected starting Friday\nwith shower chances increasing. Look for high temps to drop into\nthe 60s, possibly 50s if steadier rainshowers occur over the\nweekend.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nForecast confidence is high for strong winds today. Confidence is\nhigh north and low south for showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon and evening. Increased wind speeds early this morning in\nthe Little Rockies. Otherwise, no changes to NBM.",
